M17 Scientific Recognition: Implementation Sequence

Objective

Extend the current M17 scientific system so Ito can automatically recognize equations, molecules, and eventually reactions from selected PDF regions.

Do this incrementally. Do not replace the existing scientific representation/rendering layer. The existing system already has:

* PDF area cropping
* PDF text-layer math extraction
* LaTeX normalization
* LaTeX → MathML conversion
* SMILES / Reaction SMILES representation
* chemical mechanism representation
* deterministic chemical SVG rendering
* Word clipboard export

The new work should add a visual recognition layer that feeds these existing representations.

⸻

Phase 0 — Establish a clean baseline

Step 0.1 — Work from the latest M17 branch

Use:

m17-scientific-reflow-and-cache

Create a new feature branch:

m18-scientific-recognition

Do not modify M17 directly.

Step 0.2 — Run the existing project checks

Before changing functionality, run:

npm ci
npm test -- --run
npm run typecheck
npm run build

Record the result of each command.

If any existing test/build/typecheck fails, stop and fix the failure before implementing recognition.

Do not classify an existing failure as caused by the new feature.

⸻

Phase 1 — Test the existing scientific representation layer

Step 1.1 — Add dedicated M17 science tests

Create tests around:

src/pdf/scienceOcr.ts

Test at minimum:

Math

α
β
√x
x²
x₂
a/b
\frac{a}{b}
\sqrt{x}
\alpha

Verify:

input
→ normalized LaTeX
→ MathML

and verify that the generated MathML is structurally valid.

Chemistry

Test:

C
CC
C=C
CCO
c1ccccc1
CC(=O)O

and reaction SMILES such as:

CCO>>CC=O

Verify that parsing does not silently lose atoms or bonds.

Mechanisms

Test:

ChemicalMechanismSpec
→ SVG

with:

* atom-to-atom arrows
* bond-to-atom arrows
* full arrows
* fishhook arrows
* reaction conditions
* malformed/unresolved anchors

The renderer must fail gracefully rather than generating broken SVG.

⸻

Step 1.2 — Test the existing Area crop pipeline

Test:

PDF page
→ Area drag
→ normalized rectangle
→ imageDataUrl
→ annotation

Verify that:

1. The crop corresponds exactly to the selected visual region.
2. Normalized coordinates remain correct at different zoom levels.
3. Rotation does not shift the crop.
4. Scanned/image-only pages still produce a crop.
5. Existing text-layer pages still produce a crop.

Do not proceed until this is reliable.

⸻

Phase 2 — Repair the representation layer before adding AI

Step 2.1 — Audit the chemistry parser

Inspect the SMILES parser in:

src/pdf/scienceOcr.ts

Document exactly which SMILES features are supported.

At minimum, verify handling of:

* aliphatic atoms
* aromatic atoms
* bond orders
* ring closures
* branches
* charges
* bracket atoms
* stereochemistry
* explicit hydrogens
* disconnected components

Do not pretend unsupported SMILES are supported.

⸻

Step 2.2 — Improve chemistry representation if necessary

The renderer should preserve the chemical information that an OCSR model may produce.

Do not uppercase aromatic atoms.

For example:

c1ccccc1

must remain distinguishable from:

C1CCCCC1

Preserve:

* aromaticity
* formal charge
* atom identity
* bond order
* stereochemistry where practical

If the current renderer cannot represent a feature, extend the internal representation before connecting OCSR.

⸻

Step 2.3 — Add chemistry validation

Introduce a validation layer:

SMILES
 ↓
chemical parser
 ↓
validation result

Use a chemistry toolkit where practical, preferably RDKit if the chosen runtime architecture supports it.

The API should conceptually be:

interface ChemistryValidationResult {
  valid: boolean;
  normalizedSmiles?: string;
  errors: string[];
  warnings: string[];
}

Never silently accept an invalid recognition result.

⸻

Phase 3 — Create the recognition abstraction

Do not directly couple UI code to a particular AI model.

Create:

src/science/recognition/
    types.ts
    mathRecognizer.ts
    moleculeRecognizer.ts
    reactionRecognizer.ts
    recognitionService.ts

Define a common abstraction.

For example:

type ScienceRecognitionKind =
  | "equation"
  | "molecule"
  | "reaction";
interface RecognitionInput {
  imageDataUrl: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
}
interface RecognitionResult {
  kind: ScienceRecognitionKind;
  confidence?: number;
  rawOutput?: unknown;
}

Then make each recognizer responsible only for visual → structured conversion.

The existing rendering code remains responsible for structured → visual conversion.

Architecture:

image
 ↓
recognizer
 ↓
standard representation
 ↓
validator
 ↓
existing Ito representation
 ↓
existing renderer

Do not implement:

image → AI → ChemicalMechanismSpec

as one monolithic operation.

⸻

Phase 4 — Implement equation recognition

Step 4.1 — Select the initial math model

Use an existing image-to-LaTeX model rather than training one.

Preferred initial candidate:

pix2text-mfr

Use an inference implementation compatible with the browser/runtime architecture.

The first prototype may use a remote inference endpoint if local inference is impractical, but isolate that behind MathRecognizer.

Do not make the rest of Ito depend on the remote API.

⸻

Step 4.2 — Implement MathRecognizer

Create:

interface MathRecognitionResult {
  latex: string;
  confidence?: number;
}

Implement:

recognize(image: Blob): Promise<MathRecognitionResult>

Pipeline:

PNG crop
 ↓
math model
 ↓
LaTeX
 ↓
normalizeToLatex()
 ↓
validate/parse
 ↓
MathRecognitionResult

If the model returns invalid LaTeX, return an explicit error rather than silently storing it.

⸻

Step 4.3 — Connect equation recognition to the Area tool

Add an action to the existing Area annotation workflow:

Area selected
    ↓
Identify
    ↓
Equation

The selected imageDataUrl should be sent directly to the recognizer.

Do not re-render or re-crop the PDF unnecessarily.

⸻

Step 4.4 — Display the result for verification

Do not immediately overwrite the source crop.

Show:

Original crop
Recognized equation:
[editable LaTeX / rendered equation]
[Accept] [Edit] [Cancel]

On Accept:

AreaAnchor.latex = recognizedLaTeX

The existing MathML and Word clipboard functionality should then work without modification.

⸻

Step 4.5 — Add equation regression tests

Test several classes:

simple text
fractions
roots
superscripts
subscripts
Greek letters
integrals
summations
matrices
multi-line equations

The recognition model itself does not need to be perfectly tested in unit tests.

Test the deterministic boundary:

model output
→ normalization
→ validation
→ MathML

⸻

Phase 5 — Implement molecule recognition

Step 5.1 — Add OCSR

Use an existing optical chemical structure recognition system.

Initial candidates:

* DECIMER
* MolScribe

Prefer DECIMER if its deployment/runtime constraints fit the project.

The first implementation can use an external service if necessary.

Again, isolate it:

interface MoleculeRecognizer {
  recognize(image: Blob): Promise<MoleculeRecognitionResult>;
}

⸻

Step 5.2 — Define the result

interface MoleculeRecognitionResult {
  smiles: string;
  confidence?: number;
  warnings?: string[];
}

Pipeline:

image
 ↓
OCSR
 ↓
SMILES
 ↓
chemical validation
 ↓
normalized SMILES

⸻

Step 5.3 — Connect to Ito’s existing molecule renderer

After validation:

SMILES
 ↓
existing molecule parser
 ↓
existing SVG renderer

Show:

Original molecule
Recognized structure
SMILES:
[editable]
[Accept] [Edit] [Cancel]

On Accept:

AreaAnchor.smiles = normalizedSmiles

Do not alter the original image.

The recognized structure is an editable interpretation of the source image.

⸻

Phase 6 — Build reaction recognition

Do NOT attempt mechanism recognition yet.

First implement ordinary reaction-scheme recognition.

Step 6.1 — Segment the reaction image

The reaction recognizer needs to identify:

molecules
+
reaction arrows
+
reagent/condition text

Conceptually:

reaction image
 ↓
layout detection
 ├── molecule regions
 ├── reaction arrow
 └── text regions

Do not assume the entire crop is one molecule.

⸻

Step 6.2 — Recognize molecules independently

Each detected molecule region should pass through the same molecule recognizer:

molecule region
 ↓
OCSR
 ↓
SMILES
 ↓
validation

This avoids duplicating chemistry recognition logic.

⸻

Step 6.3 — Detect reaction direction

Represent the reaction arrow separately:

interface ReactionArrow {
  start: Point;
  end: Point;
  direction: "forward" | "reverse" | "equilibrium";
}

Determine which recognized molecule regions lie on each side.

Then construct:

reactants
agents
products

⸻

Step 6.4 — Recognize reagent/condition text

Use ordinary OCR for text such as:

H2SO4
NaBH4
heat
EtOH
Δ
hv

Do not force these through the molecule recognizer.

Store them separately as reaction conditions/agents.

⸻

Step 6.5 — Construct reaction SMILES

Generate:

reactants>agents>products

or the existing internal representation used by Ito.

Validate the resulting reaction.

Only then construct the existing ChemicalMechanismSpec.

⸻

Phase 7 — Add reaction verification UI

Show the recognized reaction as an editable object:

Original crop
Reactants:
[structure] + [structure]
Conditions:
[editable text]
Products:
[structure]
Reaction:
[editable representation]
[Accept] [Edit] [Cancel]

The user must be able to correct an incorrectly recognized molecule without starting recognition again.

⸻

Phase 8 — Mechanism recognition

Only begin this after ordinary reaction recognition is reliable.

Mechanism recognition is a separate problem.

Input:

reaction image

Output:

ChemicalMechanismSpec

but construct it in stages.

⸻

Step 8.1 — Recognize molecular structures

Reuse the molecule recognizer.

⸻

Step 8.2 — Recognize electron-pushing arrows visually

Detect:

* arrow source
* arrow destination
* arrow type
* arrow direction
* curvature

Represent initially as geometric candidates:

interface VisualElectronArrow {
  source: Point;
  target: Point;
  barb: "full" | "fishhook";
  confidence: number;
}

⸻

Step 8.3 — Map visual arrows to chemical atoms/bonds

Use the recognized molecular graph.

For every arrow:

visual source point
 ↓
nearest valid atom/bond
 ↓
chemical source
visual destination point
 ↓
nearest valid atom/bond
 ↓
chemical destination

Reject mappings that are chemically impossible.

⸻

Step 8.4 — Construct ElectronFlowArrow

Only after mapping:

ElectronFlowArrow

should be generated.

This means the existing mechanism renderer remains unchanged.

⸻

Phase 9 — Add confidence and failure handling

Every recognition result should have an explicit state:

type RecognitionStatus =
  | "success"
  | "low-confidence"
  | "invalid"
  | "unsupported"
  | "error";

Never silently produce a result when recognition failed.

For low confidence:

Recognition uncertain
[Review result]

rather than:

Accepted automatically

⸻

Phase 10 — Add a unified Identify UI

Once equation and molecule recognition work independently, add:

Area selected
    ↓
Identify
    ├── Equation
    ├── Molecule
    └── Reaction

Do not initially make automatic classification mandatory.

Manual type selection gives us a clean way to test recognition independently.

Automatic classification can be added later.

⸻

Phase 11 — Only then add automatic classification

Eventually:

Area crop
 ↓
science classifier
 ↓
equation / molecule / reaction / ordinary figure
 ↓
appropriate recognizer

This should be an optimization of the UX, not a prerequisite for recognition.

⸻

Phase 12 — Regression testing

Before merging, verify:

npm test -- --run
npm run typecheck
npm run build

and manually test:

Math

* textbook equation
* rotated equation
* scanned equation
* multi-line equation
* equation containing Greek symbols
* equation containing fractions/roots/scripts

Molecules

* simple molecule
* aromatic molecule
* charged molecule
* branched molecule
* ring system
* molecule with heteroatoms

Reactions

* simple substitution
* addition
* elimination
* multi-reactant reaction
* reagent above arrow
* conditions below arrow
* reaction containing multiple products

Existing functionality

Verify that adding recognition does not regress:

* Area annotations
* text annotations
* rotated text
* text-layer extraction
* MathML clipboard
* chemistry SVG clipboard
* mechanism rendering
* PDF scrolling
* toolbar behavior

⸻

Implementation order

The actual order must remain:

1. Baseline tests
        ↓
2. Fix existing M17 failures
        ↓
3. Test Area crop
        ↓
4. Harden chemistry representation
        ↓
5. Create recognition abstraction
        ↓
6. Equation image → LaTeX
        ↓
7. Equation verification UI
        ↓
8. Molecule image → SMILES
        ↓
9. Molecule verification UI
        ↓
10. Reaction segmentation
        ↓
11. Reaction recognition
        ↓
12. Reaction verification UI
        ↓
13. Electron-flow recognition
        ↓
14. Automatic science classification

Do not skip directly from the current M17 code to a monolithic “AI recognizes science” feature.

The design principle is:

                 VISUAL LAYER
                      │
              ┌───────┴────────┐
              ↓                ↓
         Recognition       User crop
              │                │
              └───────┬────────┘
                      ↓
             STANDARD REPRESENTATION
          ┌───────────┼───────────┐
          ↓           ↓           ↓
        LaTeX       SMILES     Reaction
          ↓           ↓           ↓
       MathML       Graph      Mechanism
          └───────────┼───────────┘
                      ↓
              EXISTING ITO RENDERERS
                      ↓
             Editable scientific
                  annotation

The recognition models are replaceable. The scientific representations and renderers should remain deterministic and under Ito’s control.